cot}⁡{50}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{80}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{9})$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521473 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "299f3633-d5e0-546a-8fb6-eacb46c1b2dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521473 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Converting ${50}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{50}^{∘}=50\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Converting ${80}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{80}^{∘}=80\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{2\\pi }{9}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and II only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521483 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.9)$ in radians?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39cecc3d-6cf0-5c7c-a0ca-fadfcdb5000c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521483 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.9)=1.119\\,769…≈1.120\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521494 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521494 #edb/ref 1069 "q-123545" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521494 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521494 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are 