\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Converting ${20}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get\n\n$$\n{20}^{∘}=20\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nThe statement is false because $\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{36}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Converting ${35}^{∘}$ from degrees to radians, we get \n\n$$\n{35}^{∘}=35\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{180}=\\frac{7\\pi }{36}\\,\\text{radians}.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is 'I and III only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521504 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521504 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521504 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521504 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to three decimal places, what is $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.71)$ in radians?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521504 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5ebed1ea-4030-56bb-a75b-85ec95570cad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521504 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.71)=0.789\\,498…≈0.789\\,\\text{radians}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521505 #edb/ref 1069 "q-54420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521505 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521505 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to $3$ decimal places, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(1)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f0cef98-4d6e-5f8f-803e-692ed6a93d90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521505 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that we need the answer in radians. Using a calculator in \"radians\" mode, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)}≈1.850\\,815…≈1.851\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218652151